    {
     "fig": "phototropism",
     "text": "Now put those four together. Light (1) shines from one side. The auxin moves to the shaded side (2) — the side away from the light. That side now has more auxin, so its cells elongate more than the cells on the lit side. One side longer than the other is a bend, and the bend points towards the light.",
     "sup": true
    },
    {
     "fig": "gravity-side",
     "text": "Gravity drives the same chain, and only the middle step changes. Lay a shoot on its side and the auxin collects along the _lower_ side (1). Those cells elongate more (2) than the ones on top, so the shoot bends _upwards_ — away from gravity. Same four steps, different stimulus, and the bend points the opposite way.",
     "sup": true
    },
    {
     "fig": "root-inversion",
     "text": "A root responds to auxin in the opposite way. In a shoot, a high concentration of auxin _stimulates_ cell elongation. In a root it _inhibits_ it: those cells elongate LESS. So when a root lies on its side, auxin collects along the lower side (1), the cells there elongate less (2) than the ones on the upper side, and the root bends downwards. The 2026–28 syllabus names only the shoot, but a question on tropisms can still show a root.",
     "ext": true
    },
    {
     "text": "The region that does the growing has a name, though the syllabus does not use it. The very tip of a shoot — and of a root, just behind its cap — is the apical meristem, where cells keep dividing to make new ones. Those new cells then lengthen in the zone just behind it, and it is that lengthening which adds most of the new length. So the bend is made by cells the meristem has already produced: they elongate. Auxin does cause cell division elsewhere in a plant, but a shoot bends because its cells get longer, not because they divide faster.",
     "ext": true
    },
    {
     "text": "And not every cell it reaches responds. Auxin travels on past the older cells further back, but those do not elongate: a cell can only stretch while its wall can still be loosened, and a mature wall cannot. So the zone just behind the tip is the only part that stretches — which is why the bend appears there and not along the whole shoot.",
     "ext": true
    },
    {
     "text": "A _clinostat_ turns the seedling slowly so that no side stays lowermost: gravity then acts equally on all sides, and any bending left is a response to something else.",
     "ext": true,
     "near": 3
    }
